<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willem Johannes Leyds</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Willem Leyds</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Willem Johannes Leyds was a Dutch-born lawyer who became state attorney of the South African Republic in 1884 and its state secretary from 1888 to 1898, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-kruger">Paul Kruger</a>'s chief adviser. He built up a professional civil service, largely staffed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> officials, and was distrusted by the mining industry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. From 1898 he represented the republic as envoy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, seeking support during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willem-johannes-leyds.jpg" alt="Willem Johannes Leyds" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Veth · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leyds-Veth.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leyds was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> teachers. After his father's death the family settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and he studied law at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, where he excelled and graduated cum laude. Some of his former lecturers recommended him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-kruger">Paul Kruger</a>, president of the South African Republic, who was touring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> after his country had regained its independence from the British in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-boer-war">First Boer War</a>. Leyds hesitated at first, but he accepted the post of state attorney and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> with his wife, Louise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As state attorney of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, Leyds handled the republic's judicial cases and took part in founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>–South African Railway Company. He soon became Kruger's right-hand man and in 1888 was promoted to state secretary. Throat trouble took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for treatment in 1896, and after his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> his recurring ill health led him to resign the office, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-william-reitz">Francis William Reitz</a> succeeded him.</p><p>From 1898 to 1902 he was the republic's special envoy and minister plenipotentiary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, accredited to several European states. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> he tried in vain to win support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> cause, and his diplomatic career ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vereeniging">Treaty of Vereeniging</a>. In 1904 he accompanied the remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-kruger">Paul Kruger</a>, who had died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, Leyds wrote a history of the South African Republic. Louise died in 1907 at the age of 52, and in 1910 he married the daughter of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> official on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. As state secretary he had received the Dutch Order of the Netherlands Lion, the German Order of the Red Eagle and the French Legion of Honour.</p><p>The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> and the University of Stellenbosch later awarded him honorary doctorates for his contribution to South African history. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Jan Smuts</a>, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> general and Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, described him as the most significant figure in the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> after Kruger. The former gold rush town of Leydsdorp in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Willem Johannes Leyds” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His government built a more professional civil service, guided from 1888 by the State Secretary <b>Willem Johannes Leyds</b>, but it also sold monopolies, notably the dynamite concession, that angered the mining industry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Magelang, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.47,110.22&amp;year=1911" aria-label="Magelang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7599 2443 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Willem Johannes Leyds"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8049 2713h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8049 2713h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.47,110.22&amp;year=1911">What was here in 1911? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
